# 01 — The simple pendulum: measuring g
#
# A mass on a string of length L swings back and forth. For small swings
# the period does not depend on the mass or on how far it swings:
#
#     T = 2π √(L / g)
#
# Turn that around and a stopwatch plus a tape measure give you g:
#
#     g = 4π² L / T²
#
# This is the first snippet of the Fermium spec (§3.1). We also compare the
# small-angle period with the exact period for a large swing, which needs an
# integral:  T = 4 √(L/g) ∫₀^{π/2} dφ / √(1 − k² sin²φ),  k = sin(θ₀/2).

L = 1.20 m
T = 2.21 s
g = 4π² L / T²
print "measured g =", g
print "in imperial:", g in ft/s²

# Period predicted with the standard value of g (g_n is built in)
T_small = 2π √(L / g_n)
print "small-angle period:", T_small

# Exact period for big amplitudes (complete elliptic integral of the first kind)
period(θ0) = 4 √(L/g_n) * ∫ 1/√(1 - sin(θ0/2)^2 sin(φ)^2) dφ from 0 to π/2

for θ0 in [5°, 20°, 45°, 90°]
    print "amplitude", θ0 in deg, " period", period(θ0), " ratio", period(θ0)/T_small

# A whole lab session: the period measured for 11 string lengths.
# The file's header says `L [cm], T [s]`, so Fermium knows the units.
# `fit` finds the g that best matches T = 2π √(L/g), starting from our g above.
data = load "data/pendulum.csv"
fit T = 2π √(L / g) to data
print "g from the fit:", g, " (standard gravity:", g_n, ")"

plot data.T vs data.L to "gallery/pendulum_data.png"
